---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
    title="Language attributes"
    metaDescription="Examples showing where to add the lang attribute when part of a page uses another language."
    currentUrl="/screen-reader-examples/lang/"
  >
  <div class="my-8 mx-4 max-w-[80em] lg:mx-auto">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[40em]">
      <h1 id="skip-target">
        Where to define the language of parts of the page?
      </h1>
      <div>
        <p>The page language is English, but sometimes a small part of the content is in another language. When that happens, add the <code>lang</code> attribute to the element that contains the language change.</p>
        <p>Descendant elements inherit the language, so you do not usually need to repeat the same <code>lang</code> attribute on every nested element.</p>

        <h2>Language on the paragraph</h2>
        <p>This example has the <code>lang</code> attribute on the paragraph. The link inside the paragraph inherits the same language.</p>

        <div class="lang-example">
          <p lang="fr">
            Voici un exemple en français avec un
            <a href="#">lien vers une ressource utile</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Language repeated on nested elements</h2>
        <p>This example repeats <code>lang="fr"</code> on the paragraph and the link. It should still work, but the language on the link is redundant because it already inherits French from the paragraph.</p>

        <div class="lang-example">
          <p lang="fr">
            Voici un autre exemple en français avec un
            <a href="#" lang="fr">lien vers une ressource utile</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lang-example {
    border: 2px solid #bbc9f7;
    margin-block: 1.5rem 3rem;
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  :global(.dark) .lang-example {
    border-color: #18399a;
  }

  .lang-example p {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }
</style>
